course: dictionary
lesson: 15879
title: Informative and uninformative
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Uninformative is a long word, but you can break it into parts. Un- means not, and informative means giving useful facts. So uninformative means giving little or no useful information.
explain: Compare two labels. «The box says only: Stuff.» tells you almost nothing, so it is uninformative. «The box says: 12 red pencils, sharpened.» tells you exactly what is inside, so it is informative.
explain: Teachers ask for informative writing. If your paragraph only says «Our trip was nice», it is uninformative, because it gives no facts about where you went or what you did. Add facts, and it becomes informative.
fact: Uninformative means giving little or no useful information.
fact: The prefix un- means not, so uninformative is the opposite of informative.
fact: «The label said only: Stuff.» is uninformative, because you cannot tell what is in the box.
fact: In science, a graph without labels is uninformative, because nobody can tell what it shows.
fact: A good check is to swap in your meaning: «The sign was uninformative» means the sign gave little useful information. If that still makes sense, you have the meaning right.
exercise: Ben answered «Yes.» to every question. What does uninformative mean about his answers? | They were very loud | They gave lots of facts | *They gave little useful information | They were written in rhyme
exercise: Which graph is uninformative? | *A graph with no labels | A graph with clear labels and a key | A graph that shows a clear pattern | A graph with a title and labels
exercise: «Her report was full of facts, so it was informative, not uninformative.» Find and copy the word that means the opposite of uninformative. | *informative | report | full | facts
test: The museum label just said «Old thing». Why is it uninformative? | It is too long to read | *It gives no useful facts about the object | It is painted in bright colours | It tells you the price
test: Which sentence about a trip is uninformative? | «The bus was yellow, with 40 seats.» | «We left at 8 am by bus.» | *«We went somewhere nice.» | «We walked 3 km to the lighthouse and saw seals.»
exam: Which word means the opposite of uninformative? | quiet | *informative | empty | heavy
